      "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nThe conventions we use in this chapter are the same as those in the\nchapter on algebraic spaces. For convenience we repeat them here.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nWe work in a suitable big fppf site <MATH_0001>\nas in Topologies, Definition <REF_0001>.\nSo, if not explicitly stated otherwise all schemes will be objects\nof <MATH_0002>. We discuss what changes if you change the big\nfppf site in\nSection <REF_0002>.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\nWe will always work relative to a base <MATH_0003> contained in <MATH_0004>.\nAnd we will then work with the big fppf site <MATH_0005>, see\nTopologies, Definition <REF_0003>.\nThe absolute case can be recovered by taking\n<MATH_0006>.\n\n<STRUCT_0006><STRUCT_0007>\nIf <MATH_0007> are schemes over <MATH_0008>, then we denote\n<MATH_0009> for the set of <MATH_0010>-valued points <TEXTITOPEN_0001>over<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> <MATH_0011>.\nIn a formula: <MATH_0012>.\n\n<STRUCT_0008><STRUCT_0009>\nNote that any fpqc covering is a universal effective\nepimorphism, see\nDescent, Lemma <REF_0004>.\nHence the topology on <MATH_0013>\nis weaker than the canonical topology and all representable presheaves\nare sheaves.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "source_text_hash": "sha256:32deaa3f1f10fa801eb8cd82e2f12e2774c948fb0cb2968bebe4c88d6c6ef318",
      "unit_id": "tag:026M:prose-0001"
    }
